e the definition of $f(x,y)?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D$ and rectangle $R:$\n\n![](images/bc/bc6d7409dee2b5935d03839d32fc3aad23f08f1dd2c65eae496df8f995960b23.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal $x+{y}^{2}$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(x+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- We cannot use function $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in D∖R\\end{aligned}\\right.$. The function is not identically zero for all points in $R\\,∖D.$ For example, $(x,y)=(2,3)\\in R\\,∖\\,D,$ but the function is nonzero at this point.\n\n- Functions $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ x+{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in D\\end{aligned}\\right.$ and $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin D\\end{aligned}\\right.$ are equals to $x+{y}^{2}$ on $D$ and equals $0$ outside $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ x+{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in D\\end{aligned}\\right.$; $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin D\\end{aligned}\\right.$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265785 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152198 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ad76e6b-acef-5221-8852-71dc42625bf3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Correct Function Used to Define a Double Integral Over a Non-Rectangular Domain" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049229 :topic/id #uuid "e52ab899-5fcb-50d2-afee-4830a70f16df" :topic/math-academy-id 2616 :topic/title "Double Integrals Over Non-Rectangular Domains"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c7dee800-c2f7-56cc-a69d-4fe5cf12dbd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.typ