nswer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463819}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1532bb77-1f2c-5080-b022-0619d674751b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463819} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1240b97-0f0b-56fb-8e7e-ea7975a78972" :answer-field/key "$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{2}^{x+y}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\right.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464843}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ef54449c-2307-5871-89d3-1b2194748b24" :question/math-academy-id "q-129909" :question/problem "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed by the curves $y=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-2$ and $y=-\\frac{1}{2}x+1.$ If $R=[-3,2]\\times [-2,3],$ and\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}{2}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of $f(x,y)?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D$ and rectangle $R:$\n\n![](images/41/414a5c8c14d70ff08d607c2a755f565c147b8b5e691a8d38a9e7db5697ba9e48.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal ${2}^{x+y}$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}{2}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- We cannot use function $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{2}^{x+y}, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in D\\end{aligned}\\right.$. The function is not identically zero for all points in $R\\,∖D.$ For example, $(x,y)=(0,3)\\in R\\,∖\\,D,$ but the function is nonzero at this point.\n\n- Functions $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{2}^{x+y}, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\right.$ and $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{2}^{x+y}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\right.$ equal ${2}^{x+y}$ on $D$ and equal $0$ outside $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{2}^{x+y}, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\right.$; $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{2}^{x+y}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\right.$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407296 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152198 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ad76e6b-acef-5221-8852-71dc42625bf3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Correct Function Used to Define a Double Integral Over a Non-Rectangular Domain" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049229 :topic/id #uuid "e52ab899-5fcb-50d2-afee-4830a70f16df" :topic/math-academy-id 2616 :topic/title "Double Integrals Over Non-Rectangular Domains"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "034d7503-b6f8-527c-a973-ac2bfc867df1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472622} {:answer/id #uuid "77d96fda-e870-55c8-b23f-4d1d5eb0e5f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472623}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "77d96fda-e870-55c8-b23f-4d1d5eb0e5f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472623} :answer-field/id #uuid "256d9380-5a22-5d58-bc62-7da50bb3ece8" :answer-field/key "$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y), & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin D\\end{aligned}\\right.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473706} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83f3b95a-f8ca-5464-96e2-8800e62a2417" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472624} {:answer/id #uuid "50306